Practical No. 1

Process Management Basics

Aim :-

Understand process creation and monitoring.

Theory :- 

A process is an active instance of a running program in Linux. 
Every process is tracked by the kernel via a unique integer called the Process ID (PID).
Process management allows users to view resource utilization and terminate frozen or unwanted tasks safely.

Requirement:

•Linux
•WSL Terminal

Command explanation :- 

a) View running process using ps and top

$ ps aux

ps aux takes a static snapshot of all active processes on the system.

$ top

top launches an interactive, real-time interface displaying resource usage dynamically.

b) Identifying Process ID (PID)

$ pgrep Firefox

$ ps aux | grep Firefox

Used to extract the unique PID of a running process needed to perform signals or tracking.

c) Kill a process using kill command

$ kill 1234

Standard kill politely asks the process to exit, saving data.

d) Use htop for interactive process monitoring

$ htop

An interactive text-based visual process viewer that expands on top by letting users sort, 
search and kill processes cleanly using shortcut keys.

